soc: mediatek: mt6797-a72: pay for the CCI overclock this bring-up performs

ARMCAXPLL2 is the CCI's PLL, not this cluster's, and mt6797_a72_set_clk()
reprograms it to 750 MHz as a prerequisite for the firmware's CPU_ON gate. So
onlining an A72 raises the interconnect from the 630 MHz the bootloader chose
and leaves it there — measured three ways:

  * the vendor maps LL/L/B/CCI to ARMCAXPLL0/1/NULL/2 (mt_cpufreq.c:5438), the
    B cluster being the one with no PLL at all;
  * with the A72s running at 1989 MHz, ARMCAXPLL2_CON1 still decodes to
    750 MHz, which cannot be clocking a cluster at 1989;
  * every pre-bring-up capture reads 0xc10c1d89 there, which is 630.4 MHz, and
    every post-bring-up one reads 0x410e6c4e, which is 750.4.

The CCI shares VPROC1 with the eight A53s, and their OPP tables floor at
1000 mV — a floor the DTS justifies *by* the CCI being at 630 MHz. The vendor's
CCI table prices 754 MHz at 1070 mV. So since the first build that onlined an
A72, every boot has run the interconnect a full operating point under its
specified voltage whenever both A53 clusters idle low, on a rail nothing told
about the change.

This holds a floor rather than pinning a voltage, and the difference is what
makes it composable: the regulator core aggregates consumers by INTERSECTION,
and mediatek-cpufreq asks for [vproc, proc_max_volt] — open at the top — so
[1070000, max] can only ever raise the rail and never restricts what cpufreq
may ask for above it. Held for exactly as long as the cluster is powered, which
is exactly as long as the CCI is at 750 MHz. Taken before
mt6797_a72_apply_prereqs(), because that is what moves the CCI.

Never fatal. A cluster running with the interconnect slightly under-volted is
what every boot has done so far; failing to improve on that is not a reason to
refuse the cores.

WHAT IT COSTS, stated rather than buried: the A53s idle at 624/650 MHz, priced
at 1000 mV, so idle VPROC1 goes up 70 mV. That is the price of the overclock
this driver already performs — it has simply been unpaid. The way to stop
paying it is to stop overclocking the CCI: restore ARMCAXPLL2 after CPU_ON,
which the firmware's gate may well allow, since it consumes the LATCHED
CLK26CALI_1 count rather than measuring anything itself. That is a separate
experiment with a hang risk — an unsatisfied gate spins forever at EL3 — and is
deliberately not attempted here.

Issue #84.

--- a/drivers/soc/mediatek/mt6797-a72.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/mediatek/mt6797-a72.c
@@ -126,6 +126,43 @@
 #define A72_VPROC2_MAX_UV	1180000
 
 /*
+ * VPROC1, the OTHER rail this bring-up moves — and it moves it without ever
+ * having said so. Issue #84.
+ *
+ * mt6797_a72_set_clk() below reprograms ARMCAXPLL2 to 750 MHz as a
+ * prerequisite for CPU_ON. ARMCAXPLL2 is the CCI's PLL, not this cluster's:
+ * the vendor maps LL/L/B/CCI to ARMCAXPLL0/1/NULL/2 (mt_cpufreq.c), and it is
+ * measurable here — with the A72s running at 1989 MHz, ARMCAXPLL2_CON1 still
+ * decodes to 750 MHz, while every pre-bring-up capture we have reads
+ * 0xc10c1d89, which is 630 MHz.
+ *
+ * So onlining an A72 raises the interconnect from 630 to 750 MHz. The CCI
+ * shares VPROC1 with the eight A53s, whose OPP tables floor at 1000 mV — and
+ * that floor is justified in the DTS *by* the CCI being at 630. The vendor's
+ * CCI table prices 754 MHz at 1070 mV. Every boot since the A72s first came up
+ * has therefore run the interconnect a full operating point under its
+ * specified voltage whenever both A53 clusters idle low.
+ *
+ * Fixed by holding a floor, not by pinning a voltage. The regulator core
+ * aggregates consumers by intersection, and mediatek-cpufreq asks for
+ * [vproc, proc_max_volt] — open at the top — so [1070000, max] composes with it
+ * and can only ever raise the rail, never restrict what cpufreq may ask for
+ * above it. Held for as long as the cluster is powered, which is exactly as
+ * long as the CCI is at 750 MHz.
+ *
+ * WHAT THIS COSTS, honestly: the A53s idle at 624/650 MHz, which their tables
+ * price at 1000 mV, so idle VPROC1 goes up by 70 mV. That is the price of the
+ * overclock this driver performs, not a new cost — it has simply been unpaid
+ * until now. The way to stop paying it is to stop overclocking the CCI:
+ * restore ARMCAXPLL2 after CPU_ON, which the firmware's frequency gate may well
+ * allow, since it consumes the LATCHED CLK26CALI_1 count rather than measuring
+ * anything itself. That is a separate experiment with a hang risk (an
+ * unsatisfied gate spins forever at EL3) and is not attempted here.
+ */
+#define CCI_MIN_UV		1070000
+#define CCI_MAX_UV		1200000
+
+/*
  * CSPM. The firmware takes this hardware semaphore around its own accesses to
  * the cluster clock dividers, and polls for it WITHOUT A BOUND. If anything
  * left it held, PSCI CPU_ON never returns and the calling CPU is stuck at EL3
@@ -214,6 +251,7 @@
 static bool a72_ready;
 static bool vproc2_on;
 static struct regulator *vproc2_reg;
+static struct regulator *cci_reg;
 static unsigned int a72_cur_mv = VPROC2_MV;
 static bool a72_cpufreq_managed;
 static int a72_set_vproc2_mv(unsigned int mv);
@@ -346,6 +384,47 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Raise the floor under VPROC1 to cover the CCI at the frequency this driver is
+ * about to put it at. See the CCI_MIN_UV comment. Never fatal: a cluster
+ * running with the interconnect slightly under-volted is what every boot has
+ * done so far, so failing to improve on that is not a reason to refuse the
+ * cores.
+ */
+static void a72_hold_cci_floor(void)
+{
+	struct device *cpu_dev;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (cci_reg)
+		return;
+
+	cpu_dev = get_cpu_device(8);
+	if (!cpu_dev)
+		return;
+
+	cci_reg = regulator_get_optional(cpu_dev, "cci");
+	if (IS_ERR(cci_reg)) {
+		ret = PTR_ERR(cci_reg);
+		cci_reg = NULL;
+		pr_warn("no \"cci\" supply for cpu8 (%d); the interconnect will run at 750 MHz on whatever the A53 tables ask for (#84)\n",
+			ret);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	ret = regulator_set_voltage(cci_reg, CCI_MIN_UV, CCI_MAX_UV);
+	if (ret) {
+		pr_warn("could not floor VPROC1 at %u uV for the CCI (%d) (#84)\n",
+			CCI_MIN_UV, ret);
+		regulator_put(cci_reg);
+		cci_reg = NULL;
+		return;
+	}
+
+	pr_info("VPROC1 floored at %u uV: this bring-up puts the CCI at 750 MHz, which the vendor prices there (#84)\n",
+		CCI_MIN_UV);
+}
+
 static int mt6797_a72_enable_vproc2(void)
 {
 	int ret;
@@ -936,6 +1015,13 @@
 		msleep(1000);
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Before the prerequisites, not after: mt6797_a72_apply_prereqs() is
+	 * what moves the CCI to 750 MHz, so the rail has to be able to carry it
+	 * first.
+	 */
+	a72_hold_cci_floor();
+
 	khz = mt6797_a72_apply_prereqs();
 
